## Why This Is Asked

Tests comprehensive data architecture skills including cross-account streaming, centralized analytics, security controls, disaster recovery, and governance at enterprise scale.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account IAM role design with least privilege principles
- Kinesis Data Streams vs MSK trade-offs for multi-tenant workloads
- Lake Formation cell-based security and column-level access controls
- Server-side encryption with KMS using per-tenant customer-managed keys
- Multi-region disaster recovery and automated schema evolution strategies

## Code Example

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "kinesis:DescribeStream",
        "kinesis:GetRecords",
        "kinesis:GetShardIterator",
        "kinesis:ListStreams"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:kinesis:*:ACCOUNT_ID:stream/TENANT_*"
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "s3:GetObject",
        "s3:ListBucket"
      ],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::central-data-lake/*",
        "arn:aws:s3:::central-data-lake"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "s3:ExistingObjectTag/TenantId": "${aws:userid}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes capabilities in cross account replication, data governance, per tenant encryption, and auditability at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Cross account IAM roles and trust policies
- S3 replication configuration with destination bucket
- KMS key policies and encryption context per tenant
- TLS enforcement and bucket policies
- Audit via CloudTrail and DynamoDB/Lambda

## Code Example

```json
{
  "ReplicationConfiguration": {
    "Role": "arn:aws:iam::A:role/ReplicateToB",
    "Rules": [
      {
        "Status": "Enabled",
        "Prefix": "tenant-id-*/",
        "Destination": {
          "BucketAccountId": "AccountB",
          "Bucket": "arn:aws:s3:::dest-bucket",
          "StorageClass": "STANDARD"
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you revoke access to a tenant quickly if misbehavior occurs?
- How would you test encryption key rotation impact on in-flight data?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to architect scalable, secure data ingestion across accounts with governance at scale, matching enterprise needs from Databricks and Salesforce.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account IAM roles and resource policies
- Lake Formation governance and Glue Data Catalog
- Per-tenant S3 isolation and partitioning strategy
- KMS key management and envelope encryption
- Audit/logging with CloudTrail, Config, and Lake Formation logs

## Code Example

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-*-data-lake/*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "aws:PrincipalTag/tenant_id": "${aws:userid}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Interview Strategy

Focus on demonstrating enterprise-scale architecture patterns, emphasizing security isolation, governance automation, and cost optimization across multi-tenant environments.

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes real-world patterns for tenant isolation, scalable quotas, and DR in multi-tenant SaaS on AWS, combining IAM TBAC, API Gateway authorizers, S3 replication, and cross-region resilience.

## Key Concepts
- IAM tag-based access control (TBAC) with aws:PrincipalTag
- Lambda authorizers extracting tenantId from JWT
- S3 bucket policies enforcing tenant-scoped prefixes
- DynamoDB-based per-tenant leaky-bucket quotas
- Cross-region replication (S3) and Global Tables (DynamoDB)
- Route 53 failover for DR

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: Lambda authorizer outline
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const token = event.headers.Authorization;
  const tenantId = decodeJWT(token).tenantId;
  return { principalId: tenantId, policyDocument: { /* allow specific actions on tenant Artifacts */ }, context: { tenantId } };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you revoke a tenant's access immediately in a shared bucket?
- How would you observe and alert on quota exhaustion across regions?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design scalable, resilient streaming pipelines on AWS, balancing compute warm-start strategies, data correctness (exactly-once), deployment safety, and observability under real-world load spikes.

## Key Concepts

- Lambda provisioned concurrency
- Kinesis Enhanced Fan-Out
- Idempotent processing and deduplication
- Dead-letter queues (DLQ)
- Canary/Blue-Green deployment with CodeDeploy
- Observability (CloudWatch, X-Ray)
- Data durability in S3 with versioning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-idempotent handler
function handle(record) {
  const id = record.eventSourceARN + ':' + record.sequenceNumber;
  if (cache.has(id)) return;
  // process
  storeRecord(record);
  cache.set(id, true);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement exactly-once semantics across DynamoDB and S3 writes?
- What metrics signal a regression after deployment, and how would you respond?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a compliant, scalable data platform with isolation and DR, beyond theory.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant isolation with Lake Formation and session tags
- Multi-region DR and S3 replication
- Ingestion pipeline (Kinesis Firehose) and Glue ETL
- Tenant-specific KMS keys and IAM role trusts
- Auditing and least-privilege access

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "s3:GetObject",
        "s3:PutObject",
        "s3:ListBucket"
      ],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/tenant-id/*",
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "ForAllValues:StringEquals": {
          "aws:RequestTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle tenant onboarding/offboarding without downtime?
- What metrics would you monitor to detect cross-region DR failovers early?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-account data governance at scale beyond single-service configs.

## Key Concepts

- Lake Formation cross-account data sharing
- IAM roles with trusted principals and external IDs
- RAM for resource sharing
- KMS cross-account encryption
- EventBridge/Glue orchestration
- Auditing and revocation testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: assume cross-account role and write a marker to shared S3
const { STSClient, AssumeRoleCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-sts");
const { S3Client, PutObjectCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-s3");

async function testAccess(roleArn, bucket, key, body) {
  const sts = new STSClient({});
  const r = await sts.send(new AssumeRoleCommand({ RoleArn: roleArn, RoleSessionName: "AuditTest" }));
  const s3 = new S3Client({ credentials: {
    accessKeyId: r.Credentials.AccessKeyId,
    secretAccessKey: r.Credentials.SecretAccessKey,
    sessionToken: r.Credentials.SessionToken
  }});
  await s3.send(new PutObjectCommand({ Bucket: bucket, Key: key, Body: body }));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test and enforce revocation across all accounts?
- How would you verify no data exposure in an incident scenario across accounts?
